Why learn to program at all
That leaves the obvious question. If an assistant can write the code, why should a student still learn to program?
The skills now worth building are:

how to use an assistant effectively, both for writing code and for managing a project;
how to assess AI-generated code critically, instead of accepting a plausible-looking result;
how to recognize where an implementation goes wrong;
how to fix what goes wrong;
how to learn a subject with an assistant’s help, rather than around it.

The third point carries particular weight in numerical work. A wrong method rarely crashes. It returns a number and draws a curve, and nothing announces the error. Judging whether the answer is right takes understanding the method well enough to know what it should produce, which is the one thing an assistant cannot supply on your behalf.

2B.1 First-order approximation: the Euler method
Here we introduce numerical methods to solve ODEs, using the constitutively expressed gene circuit from Part 2A as a running example:
\[\begin{equation}
\frac{dX}{dt} = f(X) \equiv g - kX \tag{1}
\end{equation}\]
The methods below (Euler, Heun, Runge–Kutta, backward Euler) are standard numerical integrators for ordinary differential equations; see (Hairer et al. 1993) for a thorough treatment and (Press et al. 2007) for practical implementations.
The Euler method is a first-order method for integrating ODEs (Hairer et al. 1993). Given \(X\) at time \(t\), it estimates \(X\) at the next time step \(t + \Delta t\) by
\[\begin{equation}
X(t+\Delta t) = X(t) + f(X,t)\,\Delta t . \tag{2}
\end{equation}\]
Starting from the initial condition \(X(t=0)\) and iterating Equation (2) yields \(X(t)\) at every time step. The smaller the step size \(\Delta t\), the more accurate the integration, but the more iterations (and the more compute time) are needed to simulate a fixed duration.

2B.2 Accuracy
By Taylor expansion for small \(\Delta t\),
\[\begin{equation}
X(t+\Delta t) = X(t) + \frac{dX}{dt}\Delta t + O(\Delta t^2) . \tag{3}
\end{equation}\]
The term \(O(\Delta t^2)\) collects the leading truncated contribution as \(\Delta t \to 0\) and bounds the error of the expansion. Comparing Equations (1) and (3) recovers the Euler formula (2), so the local error of the Euler method is \(O(\Delta t^2)\). The same reasoning estimates the error of other integrators.

2C.1 Exponential growth
This chapter uses ODEs to model bacterial growth (exponential growth and logistic growth) and reuses the numerical integrators developed in Part 2B. Each section works through the model and then poses a short exercise (in a highlighted box) for you to complete by editing and re-running the code. The growth models follow the standard treatment in Sontag’s lecture notes on mathematical systems biology (Sontag 2026).
The simplest model assumes a constant per-capita growth rate \(r\), so the bacterial count \(N\) obeys
\[\begin{equation}
\frac{dN}{dt} = rN , \qquad N(t=0) = N_0 . \tag{1}
\end{equation}\]
This has the analytical solution
\[\begin{equation}
N(t) = N_0 e^{rt} . \tag{2}
\end{equation}\]
(Interpret \(N\) as a continuous population, not necessarily an integer.) We first evaluate and plot the exact solution, on a linear axis and on a log-scaled axis where the exponential appears as a straight line. We then reproduce it numerically with the Euler and RK4 integrators from Part 2B (repeated here so this page runs on its own); zooming in at large \(t\), RK4 tracks the exact solution far more closely than Euler at the same step size. Finally, each language’s built-in ODE solver reproduces the same result: deSolve in R (here a fixed-step Euler) and SciPy’s solve_ivp in Python (adaptive RK45).

2D.1 Stability of steady states
Having learned how to integrate ODEs, we now ask a more qualitative question: given a gene circuit, what are its steady states, are they stable, and how many are there? We answer this graphically and analytically, and then package the answer into an effective potential, a landscape whose valleys are the stable states.
A steady state \(X_s\) of \(\frac{dX}{dt} = f(X)\) satisfies \(f(X_s) = 0\). It is stable if a small perturbation \(X \to X + \Delta X\) decays back to \(X_s\), and unstable otherwise. Linearizing \(f\) about \(X_s\) gives the criterion
\[\begin{equation}
X_s \text{ is }
  \begin{cases}
    \text{stable}   & \dfrac{df(X)}{dX}\Big|_{X_s} &lt; 0 \\[2mm]
    \text{unstable} & \dfrac{df(X)}{dX}\Big|_{X_s} \ge 0
  \end{cases} \tag{1}
\end{equation}\]
For the constitutively expressed gene from Part 2A, \(f(X) = g - kX\), there is a single steady state \(X_s = g/k\). Plotting the two terms \(g\) and \(kX\) separately, their intersection is the steady state. A small increase in \(X\) raises \(kX\) above \(g\), so \(\frac{dX}{dt} &lt; 0\) and \(X\) falls back; the state is stable (consistent with \(\frac{df}{dX} = -k &lt; 0\)).
The self-inhibiting gene, \(f(X) = g_0 + g_1 H^{inh}(X) - kX\), likewise has a single, stable steady state: \(g(X)\) decreases monotonically, so it crosses the line \(kX\) exactly once, and \(\frac{df}{dX} &lt; 0\) there. We confirm this by computing \(\frac{df}{dX}\) numerically with a central finite difference: at the steady state (around 250 nM) it is negative, so the state is stable.

3D.1 Lotka-Volterra model
This chapter models a predator-prey system, a cornerstone example of how populations regulate one another in an ecosystem (Carroll 2016), with the classic Lotka-Volterra equations (Lotka 1925; Volterra 1926; Sontag 2026). Unlike the toggle switch and chemostat of Part 3A through Part 3C, where every trajectory settled onto a fixed point, this system oscillates: its trajectories form closed orbits around a center. That difference reshapes the analysis, so beyond reusing the phase-plane tools we also look at what a center means for stability and, at the end, how to fit the model’s parameters to real population data. The general derivations (RK4, vector fields, the Jacobian stability test) are only referenced here; see Parts 3A and 3B for them.
Let \(N\) be the prey population and \(P\) the predator population. The model is
\[\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{dN}{dt} = f(N, P) = N\,(a - bP) \\[2mm]
\dfrac{dP}{dt} = g(N, P) = P\,(cN - d)
\end{cases} \tag{1}
\end{equation}\]
Prey grow exponentially at rate \(a\) and are eaten at a rate \(bP\) proportional to the predator level; predators die off at rate \(d\) and reproduce at a rate \(cN\) set by the available prey. All four parameters are positive. Throughout we take \(a = d = 1\), \(b = 0.03\), and \(c = 0.02\).

3I.3 A toggle switch with self-activating genes
Two transcription factors \(X\) and \(Y\): each inhibits the other and activates itself.





The rate equations are
\[\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{dX}{dt} = \left(g_{X0} + g_{X1}\dfrac{(X/X_{0X})^{n_{XX}}}{1 + (X/X_{0X})^{n_{XX}}}\right)\dfrac{1}{1 + (Y/Y_{0X})^{n_{YX}}} - k_X X \\[2mm]
\dfrac{dY}{dt} = \left(g_{Y0} + g_{Y1}\dfrac{(Y/Y_{0Y})^{n_{YY}}}{1 + (Y/Y_{0Y})^{n_{YY}}}\right)\dfrac{1}{1 + (X/X_{0Y})^{n_{XY}}} - k_Y Y
\end{cases} \tag{5}
\end{equation}\]
All of \(g_{X0}, g_{X1}, X_{0X}, Y_{0Y}, Y_{0X}, X_{0Y}, n_{XX}, n_{YX}, n_{YY}, n_{XY}, k_X, k_Y\) are parameters. Using any of the numerical methods from Part 3, find a parameter set for which the system has five steady states, three of them stable. For that case, choose sensible axis ranges and grid spacing, then
(1) plot the vector field (vectors or unit vectors; pick a scaling that keeps the plot legible);
(2) plot the nullclines;
(3) locate the steady states numerically;
(4) classify each steady state from the eigenvalues of its Jacobian;
(5) plot a bifurcation diagram of the steady-state \(X\) against the control parameter \(k_X\).

5C.3 Computing the total force
The force that particle \(j\) exerts on particle \(i\) points along their minimum-image separation, with the sign and magnitude set by the LJ force \(F(r_{ij})\),
\[
f_x(j \rightarrow i) = F(r_{ij}) \frac{x_i - x_j}{r_{ij}} , \qquad
f_y(j \rightarrow i) = F(r_{ij}) \frac{y_i - y_j}{r_{ij}} , \tag{2}
\]
where \(r_{ij} = \sqrt{(x_i - x_j)^2 + (y_i - y_j)^2}\) is the minimum-image distance. The total force on particle \(i\) sums the pairwise contributions from every other particle. We store all coordinates in one length-\(2N_\text{tot}\) vector, ordered \((x_1, y_1, x_2, y_2, \ldots)\), and use Newton’s third law (\(f_{j \rightarrow i} = -f_{i \rightarrow j}\)) so each pair is evaluated once.

R implementation

# force from particle j on particle i (minimum image)
force_j2i &lt;- function(i, j, x_all, a){
  xi &lt;- x_all[2*i-1]; yi &lt;- x_all[2*i]
  xj &lt;- x_all[2*j-1]; yj &lt;- x_all[2*j]
  dx &lt;- xi - xj; dx &lt;- dx - round(dx/a)*a
  dy &lt;- yi - yj; dy &lt;- dy - round(dy/a)*a
  rij &lt;- sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2)
  fij &lt;- force(rij)
  return(c(fij*dx/rij, fij*dy/rij))
}

# total force on every particle (length 2*Ntot)
force_all &lt;- function(t, x_all, a){
  Ntot &lt;- length(x_all)/2
  f &lt;- numeric(2*Ntot)
  for (i in 1:(Ntot-1)) {
    for (j in (i+1):Ntot) {
      fij &lt;- force_j2i(i, j, x_all, a)
      f[(2*i-1):(2*i)] &lt;- f[(2*i-1):(2*i)] + fij
      f[(2*j-1):(2*j)] &lt;- f[(2*j-1):(2*j)] - fij
    }
  }
  return(f)
}



Python implementation

# force from particle j on particle i (minimum image); i, j are 1-based
def force_j2i(i, j, x_all, a):
    xi, yi = x_all[2*i-2], x_all[2*i-1]
    xj, yj = x_all[2*j-2], x_all[2*j-1]
    dx = xi - xj; dx -= np.round(dx/a)*a
    dy = yi - yj; dy -= np.round(dy/a)*a
    rij = np.sqrt(dx**2 + dy**2)
    fij = force(rij)
    return np.array([fij*dx/rij, fij*dy/rij])

# total force on every particle (length 2*Ntot)
def force_all(t, x_all, a):
    Ntot = len(x_all)//2
    f = np.zeros(2*Ntot)
    for i in range(1, Ntot):
        for j in range(i+1, Ntot+1):
            fij = force_j2i(i, j, x_all, a)
            f[2*i-2:2*i] += fij
            f[2*j-2:2*j] -= fij
    return f

8C.2 Local moves and incremental energy
Metropolis-Hastings needs the change in energy for each proposed move, not the full energy. We propose a local move, displacing one randomly chosen particle by a small amount. Because only that particle’s interactions change, we recompute just the terms involving it, which costs \(O(N)\) instead of the \(O(N^2)\) of a full recomputation. This is the same idea as the incremental energy update for the Ising model in Part 8B.

R implementation

# energy change from moving particle i to x_new (only its terms change)
dp &lt;- function(x_all, a, x_new, i) {
  xi &lt;- x_all[(2 * i - 1):(2 * i)]
  ntot &lt;- length(x_all) / 2; p_old &lt;- 0; p_new &lt;- 0
  for (j in 1:ntot) {
    if (j != i) {
      xj &lt;- x_all[(2 * j - 1):(2 * j)]
      p_old &lt;- p_old + potential_ij(xi, xj, a)
      p_new &lt;- p_new + potential_ij(x_new, xj, a)
    }
  }
  p_new - p_old
}



Python implementation

# energy change from moving particle i to x_new (only its terms change)
def dp(x_all, a, x_new, i):
    xi = x_all[2 * i:2 * i + 2]
    ntot = len(x_all) // 2; p_old = 0.0; p_new = 0.0
    for j in range(ntot):
        if j != i:
            xj = x_all[2 * j:2 * j + 2]
            p_old += potential_ij(xi, xj, a)
            p_new += potential_ij(x_new, xj, a)
    return p_new - p_old

8E.4 A stochastic SIR model
Consider the epidemic reaction network (Kermack and McKendrick 1927)
\[ S + I \xrightarrow{\beta} 2I , \qquad I \xrightarrow{\nu} R , \]
with \(\beta = 0.003\) and \(\nu = 1\).
(a) Start from \(S(0) = 1000\), \(I(0) = 1\), \(R(0) = 0\). Compute the basic reproduction number \(R_0\). Run a Gillespie simulation and plot the dynamics of \(S\), \(I\), and \(R\). You can reuse the gillespie_step and ssa functions from Part 8D, supplying your own propensity and stoichiometry functions in this form:
my_propensity &lt;- function(Xs) {      # Xs: current state (plus any parameters)
  # returns a vector of propensities, one per reaction
}
my_stoichiometry &lt;- function(r_ind) {   # r_ind: the chosen reaction
  # returns the stoichiometry vector for reaction r_ind
}
(b) Run a deterministic simulation of the corresponding SIR ODEs (\(dS/dt = -\beta S I\), \(dI/dt = \beta S I - \nu I\), \(dR/dt = \nu I\)) and compare it visually with the stochastic dynamics. For the ODE integration you can use a generic RK4 routine:
RK4_generic &lt;- function(derivs, X0, n, t.total, dt, ...) {
  t_all &lt;- seq(0, t.total, by = dt); n_all &lt;- length(t_all)
  X_all &lt;- matrix(0, n_all, n); X_all[1, ] &lt;- X0
  for (i in 1:(n_all - 1)) {
    t0 &lt;- t_all[i]; t_half &lt;- t0 + dt / 2; t1 &lt;- t0 + dt
    k1 &lt;- dt * derivs(t0, X_all[i, ], ...)
    k2 &lt;- dt * derivs(t_half, X_all[i, ] + k1 / 2, ...)
    k3 &lt;- dt * derivs(t_half, X_all[i, ] + k2 / 2, ...)
    k4 &lt;- dt * derivs(t1, X_all[i, ] + k3, ...)
    X_all[i + 1, ] &lt;- X_all[i, ] + (k1 + 2 * k2 + 2 * k3 + k4) / 6
  }
  cbind(t_all, X_all)
}
(c) Now take \(\beta = 0.3\), \(\nu = 1\), and \(S(0) = 10\), \(I(0) = 1\), \(R(0) = 0\). This gives the same \(R_0\), so the deterministic dynamics are similar to part (a). Run the Gillespie simulation several times with different random seeds and compare with the deterministic result. With enough runs you should see a variety of outcomes:

dynamics like the deterministic one, with a peak in \(I(t)\) that then dies out while some \(S\) remains;
\(I(t)\) dies out immediately;
\(I(t)\) peaks but dies out early, leaving a large \(S\) population;
\(I(t)\) peaks and the epidemic consumes essentially all of \(S\).

10C.4 Boolean network models
Gene regulatory networks can be modeled without kinetic parameters by treating each gene as either on (1) or off (0) and updating its state by a logical rule of its regulators’ states (Kauffman 1969). The system state is a binary vector; in synchronous updating, every gene is updated together at each discrete time step, \(x_i(t+1) = f_i(\mathbf{x}(t))\). Iterating from any start eventually falls into an attractor: a fixed point (a steady state, \(\mathbf{x}(t+1) = \mathbf{x}(t)\)) or a cycle (an oscillation). We enumerate the attractors by running every possible initial state to its repeat, then apply this to two circuits already studied as continuous systems in Part 3H: a toggle switch and a repressilator.






NoteRecipe 10C.4.1



Objective. Enumerate the attractors of a Boolean network.
Model. A Boolean network: each gene is on or off and updates synchronously by x_i(t+1) = f_i(x(t)); iterating from any start falls into a fixed-point or cyclic attractor.
Method. Boolean network dynamics with synchronous updating, enumerating attractors exhaustively from all initial states.
Test. The generic boolean_attractors(update, n) driver, which runs every one of the 2^n start states to its first repeat.
Show. The attractors enumerated for a given update rule (applied in 10C.5 and 10C.6).
Verification. The routine returns every fixed-point and cyclic attractor, ready to apply to specific circuits.



R implementation

# enumerate attractors: run every start state to the first repeat; the cycle is the attractor
boolean_attractors &lt;- function(update, n) {
  states &lt;- as.matrix(expand.grid(rep(list(0:1), n))); found &lt;- list()
  for (r in 1:nrow(states)) {
    s &lt;- states[r, ]; seen &lt;- character(0); traj &lt;- list()
    repeat {
      key &lt;- paste(s, collapse = "")
      if (key %in% seen) {
        cyc &lt;- traj[which(seen == key):length(seen)]
        canon &lt;- paste(sort(sapply(cyc, paste, collapse = "")), collapse = "|")
        if (is.null(found[[canon]])) found[[canon]] &lt;- cyc
        break
      }
      seen &lt;- c(seen, key); traj[[length(traj) + 1]] &lt;- s; s &lt;- update(s)
    }
  }
  found
}
show_att &lt;- function(a) sapply(a, function(cyc) paste0("{", paste(sapply(cyc, paste, collapse = ""), collapse = " -&gt; "), "}"))



Python implementation

# enumerate attractors: run every start state to the first repeat; the cycle is the attractor
from itertools import product
def boolean_attractors(update, n):
    found = {}
    for s0 in product([0, 1], repeat=n):
        s = list(s0); seen = []
        while True:
            key = tuple(s)
            if key in seen:
                cyc = seen[seen.index(key):]; found[tuple(sorted(cyc))] = cyc; break
            seen.append(key); s = update(s)
    return list(found.values())
def show_att(a):
    return [" {" + " -&gt; ".join("".join(map(str, s)) for s in cyc) + "}" for cyc in a]
